# FastAPI Guidelines (Project-Specific)

Apply these rules when working with the API layer in this project (`app/main.py`, `app/routers/`, API-facing models/services).

## 1) API Role in This Project

This API service is responsible for receiving requests, validating input, and triggering processing flows.

- Keep routes thin and delegate business logic to `app/services/`.
- Do not move long-running work into routes; trigger worker/background flow instead.
- Keep persistence details in adapter/persistence layers, not in router handlers.

## 2) Router and Endpoint Design

- Use `APIRouter` with existing prefix conventions from constants (`PRIVATE_API_PREFIX`, `PUBLIC_API_PREFIX`).
- Keep endpoint naming and structure consistent with existing routers.
- Always use explicit request/response models for non-trivial payloads.
- Add clear return type hints for route handlers.
- Prefer `async def` for I/O-bound handlers; use `def` only for purely synchronous logic.

## 3) Validation and Contracts

- Use Pydantic models from `app/models/` as API contracts.
- Avoid raw dict payload handling when a model is appropriate.
- Validate input at boundary (router/model), then pass typed data to services.
- Keep response shape stable for frontend/consumer compatibility.

## 4) Error Handling and Logging

- Handle guard conditions early with clear failures.
- Use `HTTPException` for expected API errors with appropriate status codes.
- Log internal error context for debugging, but keep API error messages safe and concise.
- Avoid broad `except Exception` unless re-raising or mapping to controlled responses.

## 5) Performance and Reliability

- Avoid blocking operations in request handlers.
- Reuse existing caching and utility services (`app/services/utils/`) where relevant.
- Keep API handlers fast: validate, trigger, return.
- For async report flows, rely on status/checkpoint endpoints rather than holding request threads.

## 6) Project Consistency Rules

- Follow existing middleware and app wiring patterns in `app/main.py`.
- Keep code style compatible with Ruff and existing repository conventions.
- When endpoint behavior/path changes, update `app/routers/README.md` in the same change.
- If schema changes are needed, follow Alembic CLI workflow (do not manually craft migration files).

## 7) Cursor AI Working Style

- Read related router, model, and service files before making changes.
- Prefer minimal, focused edits over large refactors.
- Preserve backward-compatible behavior unless explicitly asked to change API contracts.
- When proposing new endpoints, mirror existing naming/versioning and response format patterns.